INT. OFFICE KITCHENETTE, MID-SIZED LOGISTICS FIRM - DAY

A fluorescent-lit kitchenette. A laminated sign over the microwave: PLEASE COVER YOUR FOOD. A second sign below it: THIS MEANS YOU. A coffee machine beeps its low-water warning.

MARGUERITE OBIORA-HASKELL (40s, cardigan, sensible shoes) stares at the coffee machine. At the table, ISAAC BRANDT-MWANGI (30s, lanyard, microwave burrito) eats without looking at anything.

The machine beeps again.

MARGUERITE
(to the room)
Does anyone know who's supposed to refill this?

ISAAC
Not me.

MARGUERITE
I didn't say you.

ISAAC
You said anyone.

MARGUERITE
I said anyone.

ISAAC
I'm part of anyone.

MARGUERITE
(muttering)
Everyone's anyone.

She lifts the carafe, peers into it, sets it down. Isaac turns a page of his newspaper. The machine beeps.

The door swings open. A man steps in, hands in his pockets: LEOPOLD ZIMMERMAN-OKAFOR (50s, suit, new badge on a new lanyard), the kind of calm that comes from a very large check.

LEOPOLD
Good morning.

MARGUERITE
Good morning.

ISAAC
Hey.

LEOPOLD
I'm Leopold. I'm the new consultant.

MARGUERITE
For what?

LEOPOLD
Efficiency.

Marguerite and Isaac exchange the longest look in the history of the break room.

ISAAC
We're very efficient.

LEOPOLD
That's what everyone says.

He walks to the coffee machine, studies it as a surgeon studies a lung, lifts the carafe, and sighs.

LEOPOLD (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Out of water.

MARGUERITE
We were just discussing that.

LEOPOLD
Who's responsible?

MARGUERITE
Nobody.

LEOPOLD
That's the problem.

ISAAC
That's the solution. If nobody's responsible, nobody's blamed.

LEOPOLD
Fascinating.

He opens a notebook. Writes. Closes it.

LEOPOLD (CONT'D)
(to the room)
I'll be observing for two weeks. Please carry on as normal.

MARGUERITE
Normal what?

LEOPOLD
Whatever you usually do.

Marguerite looks at Isaac. Isaac looks at his burrito. Neither of them moves. The machine beeps.
